   "top": {
    "Sonjabi, or handle, refers to the part attached to the body of pottery or vessels, designed to make it easier to hold or carry. Handles are created to enhance practicality and come in various shapes and sizes. They are often found on items like teapots, jars, and cups, serving both functional and decorative purposes. Handles play a significant role in traditional pottery making, reflecting thoughtful design focused on user convenience.": 2,
    "GA is the largest bronze ritual vessel for wine, featuring a body with one handle, three legs, and two knobs. It plays a significant role in traditional rituals.": 1,
    "GAGYO is a traditional double palanquin designed for two people. It was mainly used for transporting high-ranking officials or guests, often adorned with ornate decorations.": 1,
    "GAKKESURI is a traditional chest-like piece of furniture with metal decorations on the front and multiple compartments or drawers inside for storing documents or valuables, combining practicality with ornamentation.": 1,
    "The GANEUMJA is a tool used during the wheel-forming process in pottery to simultaneously measure the height and circumference of the vessel, allowing for the repeated production of works of the same size and shape.": 1,
    "GADOKGWON refers to the authority to lead a household, traditionally held by the eldest son, who would take over the responsibilities of the family line. \"Gadok\" also refers to the eldest son of the family.": 1,
    "GARABINGA is a mythical bird from Buddhist mythology, known for delivering the teachings of Buddhism with its beautiful voice. It is often depicted as a bird with a human face.": 1,
    "GARAK-BAKWI is a tool used in spinning yarn with a spindle, helping it rotate smoothly. Made from stone or clay, it plays an essential role in ensuring even spinning of the yarn.": 1,
    "GARAKJI is a type of ornament worn on the finger, typically made of materials such as metal, jade, or wood. In traditional Korean culture, it was also used as a symbol of marriage or engagement.": 1,
    "GARAKJI-JANGSIK refers to the ornamental elements added to finger rings, traditionally made from materials like metal, gemstones, or silk. It served not only as decoration but also as a symbol of status and wealth.": 1
